body:
For anyone who ran into issues on properly cleaning up a multiplayer peer in C#, this is my sollution.
```csharp
var multiplayerPeer = Multiplayer.MultiplayerPeer;
multiplayerPeer.Close();
Multiplayer.MultiplayerPeer = null;
multiplayerPeer.Dispose();
```

I've opened https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/102861 which should make sure that you only need to call `Close` and not also force the `Dispose`.

We can't really fix the issue of the C# not releasing the reference immediately, but we can at least make sure that calling `Close` will free up the UDP port even if the underlying host is being kept alive by the garbage collector.

Please if you can, test if this work:

```
var multiplayerPeer = Multiplayer.MultiplayerPeer;
multiplayerPeer.Close();
Multiplayer.MultiplayerPeer = null;
```

> Please if you can, test if this work:
> 
> var multiplayerPeer = Multiplayer.MultiplayerPeer;
> multiplayerPeer.Close();
> Multiplayer.MultiplayerPeer = null;

Just tested this using the MRP and does actually works.
Caching the peer and calling dispose on it also works.

I guess I was doing it wrong the first time I tried.
